import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.DGk0zlJw.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Store submission pre-checklist","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"store-submission.md","filePath":"store-submission.md"}'),i={name:"store-submission.md"};function n(r,e,d,c,l,p){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="store-submission-pre-checklist" tabindex="-1">Store submission pre-checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#store-submission-pre-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Store submission pre-checklist&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Beacon itself remains <strong>unpackaged and self-contained</strong> (RFC §10, <code>WindowsPackageType=None</code>) — a store submission only adds an MSIX package that wraps the whole publish output, with an in-package Identity for the store to recognize; the app&#39;s runtime shape does not change. This page records three things: how the MSIX is produced, what fields the privacy-policy page needs, and what store assets to prepare.</p><h2 id="status-2026-10-12" tabindex="-1">Status (2026-10-12) <a class="header-anchor" href="#status-2026-10-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Status (2026-10-12)&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SignPath free signing</td><td><strong>Parameters wired for real</strong> (2026-10-12): <code>api-token</code> and <code>organization-id</code> go through repository secrets (<code>SIGNPATH_API_TOKEN</code> / <code>SIGNPATH_ORGANIZATION_ID</code>, nothing hard-coded), with <code>project-slug: beacon</code> and <code>signing-policy-slug: beacon</code>; <code>artifact-configuration-slug</code> is an optional parameter — omitting it uses the project&#39;s default Artifact Configuration (fill it in later if needed, once a Portable Executable + deep signing configuration has been created in the console). <strong>One user-side step remains (not done by an agent)</strong>: in the SignPath console, add the user who owns the API token to the <strong>Submitters</strong> list of the &quot;beacon&quot; signing policy — once done, the next <code>v*</code> tag onward signs automatically; until then a version-tag build goes red at the signing step (fail closed; an unsigned official release is never shipped silently). Policy in the README &quot;Code signing policy&quot; section</td></tr><tr><td>MSIX packaging</td><td><strong>Verified on a Windows runner with byte-level package checks</strong> (2026-10-12, two green dry runs, manifest version replacement observed to happen): MakeAppx in place, the manifest accepted, a package of about 66 MB produced. The packaging logic shares <code>.github/workflows/scripts/msix-pack.ps1</code> with the nightly version-tag channel</td></tr><tr><td>Store app reservation</td><td>Not started. <code>Identity Name</code> / <code>Publisher</code> in the <code>appxmanifest</code> can only be fixed after reserving the app name in Partner Center</td></tr><tr><td>Privacy policy page</td><td><strong>Ready</strong>: <a href="https://cuihairu.github.io/beacon/privacy-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://cuihairu.github.io/beacon/privacy-policy/</a> (<code>docs/privacy-policy.md</code>, published by the docs workflow; the field mapping is in section 1)</td></tr><tr><td>Store assets</td><td>Icon prepared; screenshots / description / category / rating questionnaire not prepared (see section 2)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="_1-required-fields-of-the-privacy-policy-page" tabindex="-1">1. Required fields of the privacy policy page <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-required-fields-of-the-privacy-policy-page" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Required fields of the privacy policy page&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Store rule: an app that accesses personal data or has network capability must provide a <strong>publicly accessible</strong> privacy-policy URL. Beacon has network access (the GitHub API + AI-platform usage endpoints) and stores API tokens locally, so this is mandatory.</p><p>The page must state at least these fields (left column = the field, right column = Beacon&#39;s actual answer, copyable):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Beacon&#39;s actual answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What data is collected</td><td>API tokens (GitHub PAT, AI-platform keys); user configuration (connections, refresh intervals, theme, widget layout); local machine readings (CPU / memory / disk / network speed / IP)</td></tr><tr><td>Where the data lives</td><td>All local: tokens go into the DPAPI secret store; configuration goes to <code>%AppData%\\Beacon\\config.json</code> (only a credentialRef is stored, <strong>never a plaintext token</strong>); logs go to <code>%AppData%\\Beacon\\logs</code></td></tr><tr><td>How data leaves the machine</td><td>Sent only to the API endpoints the user configured (GitHub API, AI-platform console/usage endpoints); sent to no third party, no telemetry reporting</td></tr><tr><td>What the data is used for</td><td>Local display of state and local execution of actions only; tokens are used only to authenticate queries against the corresponding platforms</td></tr><tr><td>Retention and deletion</td><td>Removed with the install directory; uninstall through the system &quot;Add or Remove Programs&quot;; no cloud copy</td></tr><tr><td>Contact</td><td>To fill in (email or GitHub profile)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Landing requirements:</p><ul><li>[x] The public URL is ready: <code>https://cuihairu.github.io/beacon/privacy-policy/</code> (<code>docs/privacy-policy.md</code>, published by the docs workflow — submit exactly this)</li><li>[ ] Before submission the URL must already be reachable (the store crawls it)</li><li>[ ] It must not be a <code>localhost</code> / intranet address / a page that requires login</li></ul><h2 id="_2-store-asset-checklist" tabindex="-1">2. Store asset checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-store-asset-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Store asset checklist&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Beacon status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>App icon</td><td>310x310 PNG; the manifest <code>Logo</code> (small store-listing icon) uses 50x50</td><td>Ready: <code>packaging/msix/Square310x310Logo.png</code>, <code>StoreLogo.png</code> (rasterized from <code>docs/img/logo.svg</code>; the same batch also produced 44/71/150/310x150 for in-package tiles)</td></tr><tr><td>Screenshots</td><td>At least 1, 4+ recommended; PNG or JPG; common size 1920x1080 (1366x768 / 2560x1440 also accepted); per-file size limit per the submission page</td><td>Not prepared. Bases can be picked from existing evidence screenshots in <code>docs/img/evidence/</code>, but those are mostly dark-theme close-ups; light-theme, multi-screen, and settings shots are still needed</td></tr><tr><td>App description</td><td>Up to 10000 characters; cover positioning / features / supported scenarios, keywords allowed</td><td>Not prepared. Can be adapted from the README install section + the positioning section of <code>docs/mission.md</code></td></tr><tr><td>Category</td><td>Primary + secondary category, chosen from the store&#39;s list (Developer tools / Productivity are candidates)</td><td>Not decided</td></tr><tr><td>Age rating questionnaire</td><td>The IARC questionnaire answered item by item: violence, horror or fear, crude language, tobacco/alcohol, sexual content or nudity, user interaction, in-app purchases</td><td>Unanswered. Beacon has no content-risk items; the expected result is &quot;Everyone&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Support URL</td><td>Required (GitHub issues works)</td><td>Not decided</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><p>Exact size limits, allowed category values, and questionnaire wording follow the Partner Center page at submission time — the numbers in this table are the common conventions; check against the page once before submitting.</p></blockquote><h2 id="_3-msix-packaging-blockers-and-alternatives" tabindex="-1">3. MSIX packaging: blockers and alternatives <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-msix-packaging-blockers-and-alternatives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. MSIX packaging: blockers and alternatives&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The packaging chain (<code>.github/workflows/nightly.yml</code>, <code>v*</code> tag channel only):</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>publish/ (self-contained output) + packaging/msix/appxmanifest.xml (version replaced per tag)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → staging directory with AppxManifest.xml + all publish files</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → makeappx pack /d &lt;staging&gt; /p &lt;asset&gt;.msix</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → into SHA256SUMS + gh release upload</span></span></code></pre></div><ul><li><strong>Version alignment</strong>: <code>Version=&quot;0.0.0.0&quot;</code> in the manifest is a placeholder, replaced by the workflow with the four-segment form of the <code>v*</code> tag (<code>v0.1.3</code> → <code>0.1.3.0</code>); the exe&#39;s own <code>Version</code> gets the same tag version injected at the publish step, so the two are always equal.</li><li><strong>The unsigned MSIX is deliberate</strong>: the store signs it at submission (Microsoft&#39;s signature); locally it is not signed. Sideload testing needs your own development certificate (<code>makeappx pack /o</code> + a self-signed certificate) — that is a local pre-submission verification step, not done in CI.</li><li><strong>Placeholders that must be replaced before submission</strong>: <code>Identity Name=&quot;Beacon.CuiHairu&quot;</code> → the app name reserved in Partner Center; <code>Publisher=&quot;CN=cuihairu&quot;</code> → the submission certificate subject (consistent with <code>PublisherDisplayName</code>).</li><li><strong>Windows runner field test (2026-10-12, two green dry runs: 38230149697 + 38233094172)</strong>: <ul><li>MakeAppx is present on the runner: <code>C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Windows Kits\\10\\bin\\10.0.26100.0\\x86\\makeappx.exe</code></li><li>The hand-written manifest was accepted by MakeAppx (namespaces / <code>uap:VisualElements</code> / <code>uap:DefaultTile</code> / <code>rescap:runFullTrust</code> all valid); packing completes in about 10 s</li><li>Package-level byte verification (artifact downloaded and unpacked locally): the zip container is valid, about 66 MB / 502 entries; <code>AppxManifest.xml</code> + 6 Logos are all at the package root with byte counts matching the repository files one by one; <code>Beacon.App.exe</code> and <code>Assets\\</code> entered the package from publish</li><li>The version-replacement path was observed live (run 38233094172, the dry-run harness deliberately passed 9.9.9.9 ≠ the placeholder): the in-package Identity actually became <code>Version=&quot;9.9.9.9&quot;</code> and the placeholder <code>0.0.0.0</code> disappeared — the &quot;version aligned to the <code>v*</code> tag&quot; mechanism is proven to really happen, not an empty replacement</li></ul></li><li><strong>The only blocker at the time was &quot;no way to dry-run&quot;</strong>: the MSIX step runs only in the <code>v*</code> tag channel, and version tags are given manually by the author (a repository red line: no tags); dry-running with an existing tag would upload another commit&#39;s assets onto an already-published release, violating delivery integrity. The solution was to extract the shared script <code>.github/workflows/scripts/msix-pack.ps1</code> plus a dry-run harness <code>.github/workflows/msix-dry-run.yml</code> that only builds the package and never touches the release or tags.</li></ul><p>If the packaging chain breaks in the future, alternatives (in recommended order):</p><ol><li><strong>Sparse package</strong> (<code>WindowsPackageType=Sparse</code>) — the WinUI 3-supported mode of &quot;has package identity but still runs unpackaged&quot;: add <code>WindowsPackageType=Sparse</code> + <code>PackageCertificateKeyFile</code> to the csproj and the appxmanifest is generated by MSBuild automatically (no hand-writing). Cost: local development and the portable form would have to switch mode along with it.</li><li><strong>App Installer distribution</strong> (<code>.appinstaller</code>) — an enterprise / personal distribution channel that bypasses the store.</li><li><strong>Zip + setup.exe only</strong> — the store is not a distribution requirement: the portable zip + Inno Setup installer already cover personal distribution; MSIX exists purely for listing.</li></ol>`,19)])])}const g=t(i,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
